Script to present the slide
“Once the SMS delivery report is generated using the Python script, the next step is to load that report into the SMS response table.
This Alteryx workflow reads the generated SMS Response CSV file, cleans and standardizes the data, joins it with the required member information, and finally loads the processed records into the database.
The main purpose of this step is to make the delivery-response information available in a structured table for reporting, auditing, and downstream analysis.”
Then transition to the workflow:
“I’ll now walk through the workflow at a high level.”
Workflow explanation
1. Read the SMS response report
Show the Input Processing container.
Say:
“The workflow starts by reading the SMS Response Report generated by the Python script.
The Directory tool looks for files matching the SMSResponse_Report naming pattern in the configured folder.
This allows the workflow to identify and process the correct report file without manually selecting every file.”
The file specification visible in your screenshot is similar to:
SMSResponse_Report_*.csv
2. Enter the monthly manual values
Show the Manual Entries container.
Say:
“The next section contains the monthly manual values.
Here, we update information such as the User Story and Program Name for the current campaign month.
These fields are later added to the delivery-response records so that each record can be linked back to the correct campaign.”
From your screenshot, examples include:
USER_STORY: US10078451
PROGRAM_NAME: SMS OUTREACH
You do not need to read the actual user-story number during the presentation. You can just say that it is updated for each monthly run.
3. Join with member reference data
Show the member query and the Join tool.
Say:
“The workflow also retrieves member reference information from the member-report-month table.
It uses the GMPI ID as the matching key to join the response report with the member data.
This allows us to add any required member and campaign-related fields that may not be present in the original API response file.”
Your screenshot shows the join using:
GMPI_ID = GMPI_ID
4. Combine the available records
Show the Union tool.
Say:
“After the join, the workflow combines the required output streams using the Union tool.
The union is configured by field name so that matching columns are aligned correctly before further processing.”
5. Convert timestamps to Central Time
Show the formula section labeled Convert DateTime to CST.
Say:
“The API response timestamps are originally returned in UTC.
In this step, the workflow parses those timestamp fields and converts them into Central Time.
This keeps the database dates aligned with the business reporting timezone.”
You can mention examples such as:
Created date
Sent or scheduled date
Delivered date
Updated date
Avoid saying every field unless the audience asks.
6. Standardize file information
Show the filename formula.
Say:
“The workflow also standardizes the file name and captures the file-creation date.
Any folder path or unwanted characters are removed so that only the actual source file name is stored in the table.
This helps us identify which source file created each database record.”
7. Clean the data
Show the Data Cleanse container.
Say:
“Next, the data is cleaned before loading.
Leading and trailing spaces, tabs, line breaks, and duplicate spaces are removed.
This prevents formatting inconsistencies in member names, phone numbers, and response-status fields.”
8. Convert text values such as NULL into database nulls
Show the Multi-Field Formula tools.
Say:
“Some values from the CSV may contain the word NULL as text, or may be completely empty.
The workflow converts these values into proper database null values.
This is applied to fields such as phone number, names, API response status, service response status, and other report columns.”
A simple way to explain the logic:
“Empty values and text values containing NULL are standardized as true nulls.”
9. Validate whether the file was already loaded
Show the Check File container.
Say:
“Before loading the records, the workflow checks the target table to see whether the same file has already been processed.
This validation helps prevent the same response report from being loaded more than once.”
This is an important control point, so pause slightly here.
10. Sort and prepare the final dataset
Show the sort/select steps before the output.
Say:
“The final records are then arranged in the required field order and sorted using the delivery-date field.
Only the fields required by the target table are selected for loading.”
11. Bulk-load into the database
Show the Bulk Load to SQL Server container.
Say:
“In the final step, the cleaned and validated records are bulk-loaded into the SMS response database table.
Bulk loading is used because it is more efficient when processing multiple delivery-response records.
Once the load is completed, the SMS delivery details are available for reporting and campaign analysis.”
Your screenshot says SQL Server, although some other screens appear to show Snowflake-related connections. During the presentation, use the exact name of the actual target platform in your environment. A safe line is:
“The final records are loaded into the SMS response target table.”
Full casual presentation script
“This step is SMS Text 06.1, where we load the SMS Delivery Response Report.
The input for this workflow is the response CSV generated by the Python script in the previous step.
The workflow first searches the configured folder for the latest SMS Response Report file. We also update the monthly manual values, such as the User Story and Program Name.
The report is then joined with the member reference data using GMPI ID so that all required member and campaign information is available.
After that, the data is standardized. The API timestamps are converted from UTC to Central Time, the source file name and file-creation date are captured, and formatting issues such as extra spaces and line breaks are removed.
The workflow also converts empty values and the text value NULL into proper database nulls.
Before loading, it checks whether the same file has already been processed. This prevents duplicate file loads.
Finally, the required fields are selected, the records are sorted, and the response data is bulk-loaded into the SMS response target table.
Once this is completed, the message-delivery status is available for reporting, auditing, and further campaign analysis.”
